    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **James Wood** anchoring the lineup as our primary engine. Sporting an elite 78.42 score, **James Wood** benefits from a high-upside matchup against Merrill Kelly (xERA 4.74) and a lead-off spot that maximizes his stolen base potential against a pitcher with a slower-than-average delivery. He is joined in the \"Elite\" tier by **Brice Turang**, whose 73.40 rating is heavily buoyed by a favorable matchup against Cal Quantrill (implied via Feltner) and a high stolen base probability, alongside **Seiya Suzuki**, who earns a 70.94 score thanks to a favorable wind-out condition and a significant platoon edge against Robbie Ray. While **Vinnie Pasquantino** remains a \"Strong\" start at 62.07, he carries the day's primary risk, as his matchup against Jake Matthews is threatened by a 38% rain probability that warrants close monitoring before first pitch.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Zach McKinstry** on the bench. With a score of 38.03, he fell just short of our 40.0 \"Below Floor\" threshold. His case was hurt significantly by facing Bryan Woo, whose 3.45 xERA is one of the toughest marks on today\u2019s board, and a bottom-of-the-order assignment that suppresses his counting stat potential. We continue to manage our bench depth\u2014including **Trevor Story**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Max Muncy**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Alex Freeland**, **Kyle Karros**, and **Carson Williams**\u2014by prioritizing these daily efficiency scores to preserve our remaining game caps. Meanwhile, **Corbin Carroll** (61.72) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (65.86) provide essential stability; **Tyler Soderstrom** specifically benefits from a strong platoon advantage against Peyton Lambert, helping to offset the defensive drag created by the opposing catcher's pop time metrics. We are proceeding without **Mickey Moniak**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, and **Jose Caballero**, who remain sidelined due to injury or absence from the confirmed lineup.",
